# Physics

Forces and torques — only in `FixedUpdate`. Nothing physical in `Update`.

Segment size is set by sprite size and collider size, not by scale:

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — distorts physics and child objects
transform.localScale = new Vector3(size.x, size.y, 1f);

// ✅ GOOD
sprite.drawMode = SpriteDrawMode.Sliced;
sprite.size = size;
collider.size = size;
```

The X axis is “forward–back”, not “left–right”. There is no separate lateral axis in
the sagittal model, so you cannot spread paired limbs along X:
`hipHalfSpacing` and `shoulderHalfSpacing` enter the joint anchors, and the human
will stand in a real lunge. Spread only the picture — the sprite lives on
the child `Visual`, physics on the segment itself:

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — shifts the body; that is a step forward, not a viewpoint
thigh.transform.localPosition = new Vector3(sideOffset, y, 0);

// ✅ GOOD — only what is drawn moves
thigh.SetVisualOffset(sideOffset);
```

Check such a change with a run: a purely visual change must give
bitwise the same CSV as before it.

Torque is always applied to both bodies of the joint (Newton’s third law):

```csharp
rbSelf.AddTorque(torque, ForceMode2D.Force);
joint.connectedBody.AddTorque(-torque, ForceMode2D.Force);
```

For joint control use antagonist muscles (`direction` +1/-1),
not `HingeJoint2D.useMotor`.

# Code

New comments in English. Do not mass-rewrite existing C# comments:
explain why and the physical meaning, not restate the line.

Look up joints, muscles, and `Transform` once in `Awake`, not
every frame through `transform.Find` or `GetComponents`.

Check `null` before use: the body is assembled dynamically, and
a segment may be missing.

Put parameters in public fields with `[Header]` and do not overwrite them
hard in `Start` — otherwise the Inspector is useless.
